{"name":"Luna Light","hex":"#c2ceca","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"luna","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A luna moth: a member of species Actias luna.","A lunette: a crescent-shaped receptacle, often glass, for holding the (consecrated) host (the bread of communion) upright when exposed in the monstrance. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A foreman on a plantation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/luna"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Luna (goddess)","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Roman religion and mythology, Luna is the divine embodiment of the Moon. She is often presented as the female complement of the Sun, Sol, conceived of as a god. Luna is also sometimes represented as an aspect of the Roman triple goddess, along with Diana and either Proserpina or Hecate. Luna is not always a distinct goddess, but sometimes rather an epithet that specializes a goddess, since both Diana and Juno are identified as moon goddesses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Luna-MAHG_MF_1317-P8050445-gradient.jpg/330px-Luna-MAHG_MF_1317-P8050445-gradient.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luna_(goddess)","wikidata":"Q1235172"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined 